.TH mbmesh 1 "7 September 2026"
.SH NAME
mbmesh \- reconstruct triangle meshes from swath bathymetry data
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbmesh\fR \fB-I\fI datalist\fR [\fB-O\fI outputdir\fR]
[\fB-R\fI west/east/south/north\fR] [\fB-L\fI meters\fR]
[\fB--info\fR] [\fIoutput options\fR] [\fB-V\fR] [\fB-H\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
\fBmbmesh\fR reads swath sonar data through the MB-System datalist
processing framework and reconstructs a triangle mesh from accepted
bathymetry soundings. The program estimates local point spacing, derives
default reconstruction settings, estimates oriented normals, reconstructs a
signed scalar field with screened Poisson, extracts a surface with marching
cubes, trims unsupported mesh regions, and writes the result as a binary glTF
mesh.
.LP
The output mesh vertices are WGS84 Earth-Centered, Earth-Fixed (ECEF)
coordinates in meters, following the conventions of \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR. Without
\fB--geoorigin\fR the vertices are absolute ECEF coordinates. With
\fB--geoorigin\fR they are offsets from the ECEF position of a GeoOrigin; the
axes stay aligned with ECEF. An \fBmbmesh\fR product and an \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR
product made with the same GeoOrigin therefore line up directly. GLB positions
are 32-bit floats, so absolute ECEF coordinates resolve only about 0.5 m; use a
GeoOrigin to preserve finer detail.
.LP
Reconstruction itself runs in a local east-north-up frame tangent to the WGS84
ellipsoid at the point below the centroid of the accepted soundings. Soundings
are converted to ECEF and rigidly translated and rotated into this frame, so no
map-projection distortion is introduced, and results are converted back to ECEF
exactly on output. This frame does not depend on \fB--geoorigin\fR.
.LP
The default output is:
.RS
.TP
.B mesh.glb
Final triangle mesh in binary glTF format using the \fBmbmesh\fR local
Cartesian coordinate frame.
.RE
.LP
Point cloud, diagnostic, and HTML viewer files are written only when requested
with command-line output options.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-I\fI datalist\fR, \fB--input\fR \fIdatalist\fR
Input MB-System datalist file. This option is required unless the datalist path
is supplied as the first non-option argument.
.TP
.B \-O\fI outputdir\fR, \fB--output\fR \fIoutputdir\fR
Output directory for generated files. The directory is created if needed.
The default is \fImbmesh_output\fR.
.TP
.B \-R\fI west/east/south/north\fR, \fB--bounds\fR \fIwest/east/south/north\fR
Restrict accepted soundings to a geographic bounding box in degrees longitude
and latitude.
.TP
.B \-L\fI meters\fR, \fB--lod\fR \fImeters\fR, \fB--level-of-detail\fR \fImeters\fR
Requested smallest feature size in local meters. The Poisson cell is a quarter
of it, but no finer than half the point spacing, so \fB-L 0.2\fR gives a 5 cm
cell even where the soundings are much denser. If omitted, \fBmbmesh\fR
derives level of detail from the measured point spacing (twice the spacing),
raised if needed to keep the reconstruction within about 500 million Poisson
cells across the survey, and logs that it did so. A level of detail given here is
never limited, but a warning is printed when it needs more than about 2 billion
cells, which may take hours. The implementation clamps the resolved value to a
supported range and uses it to derive default normal-estimation, Poisson, and
trimming parameters. Use \fB--info\fR to see the resulting cell size and
tile plan before running.
.TP
.B \--geoorigin[=\fIlon,lat,elev\fR], \-G[\fIlon,lat,elev\fR]
Write vertices as ECEF offsets from a GeoOrigin, with the same syntax and meaning
as in \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR. With a value, longitude and latitude in decimal degrees
and elevation in meters above the WGS84 ellipsoid are all required. With no
value, the GeoOrigin is the center of the data bounds at the mean sounding
elevation. If the option is not given, vertices are absolute ECEF coordinates.
GLB positions are 32-bit floats, so absolute ECEF (magnitudes of about 6.4
million meters) resolves only about 0.5 m; use a GeoOrigin to preserve
sub-meter detail.
As in \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR the value must be attached to the option: for the long
form write an equals sign and no spaces, as in \fB--geoorigin=-122.4,36.6,0\fR;
for the short form put the value directly after \fB-G\fR, as in
\fB-G-122.4,36.6,0\fR. A value given as a separate argument, such as
\fB--geoorigin -122.4,36.6,0\fR, is not accepted, and neither is a value with
spaces unless it is quoted so that the shell passes it as one argument. With
\fB--html\fR, the viewer page positions the model with GeoOrigin and
GeoViewpoint nodes.
.TP
.B \--chunk-size \fImeters\fR
Reconstruct in square horizontal tiles of about this size (rounded up to a whole
number of Poisson cells) instead of in one pass. Chunking turns on
automatically when a single Poisson grid would exceed about 8 million cells.
See \fBChunked reconstruction\fR below.
.TP
.B \--chunk \fIi,j\fR
Reconstruct only tile (i,j). Tile (i,j) covers x from i*size to (i+1)*size and y
from j*size to (j+1)*size in the local east-north frame. Intended for testing.
.TP
.B \--max-chunks \fIn\fR
Stop after n tiles. Intended for testing.
.TP
.B \--threads \fIn\fR
Number of tiles reconstructed at the same time when chunking. The default is the
smaller of the number of processor cores and 4. Each tile holds its own Poisson
grid, so memory use grows with this value. With automatic tiling, a larger value
also gives smaller tiles, since at least two tiles per thread are wanted. The
output does not depend on it.
.TP
.B \--decimate \fImeters\fR
Enable voxel-grid point decimation with the specified cell size in local meters.
The legacy single-dash form \fB-decimate\fR is also accepted.
.TP
.B \--info
Print dataset information and the reconstruction plan, and exit before mesh
reconstruction. This includes input counts, geodetic bounds, the estimated point
spacing, and the resulting Poisson cell size, tile plan and cell count. This
option was called \fB--metadata\fR in earlier versions; \fB--metadata\fR is no
longer accepted.
.TP
.B \-V, \--verbose
Enable progress and diagnostic logging. Messages use the same line format as
\fBmbgrd2gltf\fR: level, local date and time, source file, function and line,
then the message, for example
\fBINFO 2026-10-19 14:35:15.419 mbmesh.cpp main():151 [startup] ...\fR.
Without \fB-V\fR, INFO, WARN and ERROR lines are shown; \fB-V\fR adds DEBUG
lines with per-stage and per-tile progress. All messages go to standard error.
.TP
.B \-H, \-h, \--help
Display help message and exit.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.LP
Every long option in this section also accepts a legacy single-dash form
(e.g. \fB-html\fR, \fB-diagnostics\fR, \fB-all-outputs\fR) for backward
compatibility.
.TP
.B \--html
Also write \fBmesh.html\fR, an X3DOM viewer page that references
\fBmesh.glb\fR, and print the command to serve and view it. \fBmbmesh\fR does
not start a web server or a browser; run the printed command
(\fBpython3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory\fR \fIoutputdir\fR)
in a terminal, press Ctrl-C to stop it, and open the printed URL.
.TP
.B \--xyz
Also write both local and ECEF XYZ point cloud files.
.TP
.B \--local-xyz
Also write \fBpointcloud-local.xyz\fR, containing accepted and decimated
soundings in the local Cartesian coordinate frame used for reconstruction.
This is the same coordinate frame used by \fBmesh.glb\fR.
.TP
.B \--ecef-xyz
Also write \fBpointcloud-ecef.xyz\fR, containing accepted and decimated
soundings in WGS84 Earth-Centered, Earth-Fixed coordinates.
.TP
.B \--oriented-ply
Also write \fBoriented-pointcloud-ecef.ply\fR, an oriented point cloud suitable
for inspection.
.TP
.B \--pointcloud-glb
Also write \fBpointcloud.glb\fR, a diagnostic GLB point cloud for smaller
datasets.
.TP
.B \--normal-glb
Also write \fBnormals.glb\fR, diagnostic normal vectors for smaller datasets.
.TP
.B \--origin-glb
Also write \fBorigins.glb\fR, diagnostic sensor-origin rays for smaller
datasets.
.TP
.B \--track-glb
Also write \fBtrack.glb\fR, the sensor track as a 3D line, with one position per
ping. The positions are in the same coordinates as \fBmesh.glb\fR, including any
\fB--geoorigin\fR offset, so the two overlay directly.
.TP
.B \--raw-mesh-glb
Also write \fBraw_mesh.glb\fR, the untrimmed triangle mesh produced by marching
cubes before support trimming.
.TP
.B \--diagnostics
Also write \fBpointcloud.glb\fR, \fBnormals.glb\fR, and \fBorigins.glb\fR.
.TP
.B \--all-outputs
Write all optional output files.
.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
Reconstruct a mesh and write the default \fBmesh.glb\fR:
.RS
.B mbmesh -Idatalist.mb-1
.RE
.LP
Write the mesh, X3DOM viewer, and local/ECEF XYZ point clouds:
.RS
.B mbmesh -Idatalist.mb-1 -O output/mbmesh_run --html --xyz -V
.RE
.LP
Restrict processing to a geographic region and request a coarser level of
detail:
.RS
.B mbmesh -Idatalist.mb-1 -R-122.5/-121.8/36.5/37.2 -L 0.5 -V
.RE
.LP
Print dataset information and the reconstruction plan without reconstructing a mesh:
.RS
.B mbmesh -Idatalist.mb-1 --info
.RE
.SH OUTPUT FILES
.LP
\fBmbmesh\fR always writes \fBmesh.glb\fR. Optional files are written only when
their corresponding output options are supplied.
.TP
.B mesh.glb
Final trimmed triangle mesh in binary glTF format. Vertex positions are ECEF
coordinates, or ECEF offsets from the GeoOrigin when \fB--geoorigin\fR is
used. When reconstruction is chunked this is the merged mesh of all tiles.
.TP
.B tiles/tile_I_J.glb
Written only when reconstruction is chunked: one file per tile, with vertices
placed exactly as in \fBmesh.glb\fR.
.TP
.B raw_mesh.glb
Optional untrimmed triangle mesh before support trimming.
.TP
.B mesh.html
Optional X3DOM preview page referencing \fBmesh.glb\fR. When \fB--html\fR is
used, \fBmbmesh\fR prints the command to serve this page from the output
directory; browsers will not load \fBmesh.glb\fR from a file URL.
.TP
.B pointcloud-local.xyz
Optional XYZ point cloud in the same local Cartesian coordinate frame as
\fBmesh.glb\fR.
.TP
.B pointcloud-ecef.xyz
Optional WGS84 Earth-Centered, Earth-Fixed XYZ point cloud.
.TP
.B oriented-pointcloud-ecef.ply
Optional oriented ECEF point cloud.
.TP
.B pointcloud.glb
Optional diagnostic point cloud GLB.
.TP
.B normals.glb
Optional diagnostic normal-vector GLB.
.TP
.B origins.glb
Optional diagnostic sensor-origin-ray GLB.
.TP
.B track.glb
Optional sensor track, drawn as line segments through the sensor position of each
ping that contributes an accepted sounding. The track is broken where the swath
file changes, where the time runs backwards, and where the time between two pings
is more than ten times the median ping interval of the file, so that it does not
run straight across the turns between survey lines or across gaps in the data.
.SH PROCESSING NOTES
.LP
.B Sounding filtering
.br
\fBmbmesh\fR uses MB-System datalist processing and accepts valid bathymetry
soundings. Geographic filtering is applied when \fB-R\fR is specified.
.LP
.B Coordinate frame
.br
\fBmbmesh\fR converts each sounding and sensor position to WGS84 ECEF
coordinates and then to a local east-north-up frame (see above). Sounding
elevation is taken as negative bathymetry, and sensor elevation as negative
sensor depth, both treated as heights above the WGS84 ellipsoid. The optional
ECEF point-cloud outputs apply the exact inverse transform.
.LP
.B Chunked reconstruction
.br
A single screened-Poisson grid covers the bounding box of its input, so memory
and run time grow quickly with survey size and level of detail. In chunked mode
the survey is divided into square horizontal tiles on a fixed lattice anchored at
the local-frame origin. Each tile is reconstructed from its own points plus a
surrounding halo of neighboring points, on a Poisson grid aligned to a lattice
shared by all tiles, and keeps only the triangles whose centroids fall inside
it. Tiles are reconstructed in parallel and delivered in tile order. All
soundings are still read into memory before tiling, and tile edges are not
welded, so small steps or cracks can occur where tiles meet.
.LP
.B Automatic settings
.br
The program estimates point spacing from nearest-neighbor distances inside blocks
of consecutive soundings, which does not depend on the number of soundings, and derives
default normal-estimation, screened-Poisson, marching-cubes, and support
trimming settings from the data and requested level of detail.
.LP
.B Support trimming
.br
Unsupported mesh regions are removed after marching cubes. If trimming removes
the entire mesh, \fBmbmesh\fR exits with an error rather than writing an empty
surface.
.LP
.SH MB-SYSTEM INTEGRATION
.LP
\fBmbmesh\fR integrates with the MB-System datalist processing framework and can
read swath files in MB-System supported formats.
